  ☐ Inventory the full wrapper hierarchy, partial files, reference values and helper chains for concrete
    cache/backend types, reader selection, native diagnostics, capability access, fully qualified calls,
    aliases and indirect execution. Explicitly classify backend-owned OpenGLPassTarget and private geometry
    recipients by callers and ownership; directory names/internal visibility alone do not justify an exemption.
  ☐ Update every affected production, generated-binding and test caller directly, and remove superseded
    APIs and imports in its owning correction unit. No forwarding facade, compatibility alias, duplicate
    registry, dispatcher, cache or delayed shim-removal task is permitted.

Phase 8g: Remove legacy state purges and complete intercepted state propagation
  Depends on 8a–8f.

  ☐ Remove GpuUniformRingBeginRenderer.OnRenderFrame's OpenGlStateCache.BeginFrame call and delete the
    cache BeginFrame/PurgeCache methods once their callers are removed. Keep controller.BeginFrame and
    uniform allocation/publication epochs intact. Frame transitions do not invalidate executed binding,
    framebuffer-routing or pixel-pack knowledge; do not replace the purge with an RHI forwarding operation.
  ☐ Remove ScreenResourceManager.HandleScreenResize's InvalidateAll. Preserve known state across ordinary
    resize and retain only affected resource retirement/replacement or explicit window-surface effects.
    Distinguish resizing from context recreation; no blanket resize recapture or duplicate knowledge store.
  ☐ Reconcile VanillaGraphicsExpandedModSystem.StartClientSide's InvalidateAll with context admission.
    Verify initialization order and initial unknown knowledge, then remove the duplicate reset; establish
    context identity before cached state is consumed rather than depending on a caller-owned startup purge.
  ☐ Remove GpuFramebuffer.OnDetached's routing invalidation when only wrapper ownership transfers.
    Remove OnBeforeDelete's duplicate invalidation in favor of the retirement/deletion authority, preserving
    queued original-context retirement and the distinction between logical retirement and native deletion.
  ☐ Remove unconditional routing invalidation from PublishRenderPassMetadata and RefreshWrappedFramebuffer
    when attachment publication or observed replacement provides no evidence that routing became unknown.
    Preserve routes for still-live original associations; deletion/name reuse must never reuse an old route.
    Do not retain targeted recovery merely because another mod may have changed routing: runtime GL
    mutations must already be observed. Retain acquisition only for genuinely unavailable initial facts
    or justified operation outcomes under the approved query policy.
  ☐ Inventory remaining broad resets and recapture callers, including indirect helper chains, and classify
    their actual cause. Assume complete GL interception across project, engine and third-party mod callers.
    Audit EngineStateTargets' selected-assembly rewriting and all other interception entry paths against
    this invariant; close coverage gaps, including calls from other mods, as correctness bugs in this unit.
    Do not preserve an unobserved-runtime-mutation recovery mode or use blanket purges to mask missing hooks.
    Retain explicit acquisition for initially unknown facts and knowledge reset on context identity changes.
  ☐ Inventory interception handlers that forget affected state and later query it instead of propagating
    command effects. Include BeginNativeFramebufferRouting/NativeFramebufferRouting.Complete,
    OpenGLPixelStoreCalls.InvalidatePixelTransferLayout and analogous binding, selector, fixed-function
    and resource-configuration handlers. Record each forgotten field, its command inputs/defined effects,
    downstream recapture and whether any genuine outcome uncertainty requires acquisition.
  ☐ Replace unnecessary field-level invalidation/readback with direct updates to the existing authoritative
    cache or manager snapshot from intercepted arguments, guaranteed defaults and defined derived effects.
    Cover framebuffer draw routing, integer/float pixel-store semantics, binding aliases and indexed/object-
    local effects; preserve exact overload arguments/errors, ordering, context identity and exactly-once
    dispatch/publication. Do not assume native return alone proves acceptance or publish guesses after failure.
    Use the approved operation/query policy without universal GetError/Is* probes, retry, a second cache
    or requested/actual duplicate metadata. Complete each handler and dependent reader migration together.
  ☐ Consolidate resource deletion handling into its single observed retirement/deletion path. Preserve
    logical-versus-native retirement and remove only affected bindings/routes/dependencies; wrapper cleanup
    must not discard unrelated knowledge or treat detach as deletion. Never reuse facts after name reuse.
  ☐ Retain only justified uncertainty after failed commands/restoration, genuinely unavailable initial
    facts, context identity changes and outcomes that cannot be established under the approved query policy.
    Document the reason at remaining invalidation/acquisition sites; current invalidate-and-query behavior
    is not itself justification for retaining it. Preserve boundary snapshots/restoration, reuse known
    values and query only genuinely missing facts; another mod's observed mutation is not missing knowledge.
  ☐ Run delegated focused regressions showing known bindings/routing/layout survive frame boundaries and
    ordinary resize without repeat state queries, while uniform epochs still advance. Cover startup order,
    genuine context switches, initially unknown state, GL calls originating in other mods, detach, queued
    retirement, deletion/name reuse and failed restoration. Verify those calls update the same cache without
    purge/recapture. Include intercepted routing, pixel-store and binding changes followed by boundary
    capture/restore and repeated operations: assert correct native state, no avoidable readback and preserved
    suppression. Exercise rejected/failed commands to ensure truthful cache knowledge and unchanged errors.
    Use existing diagnostic/test seams; no new counters, benchmarks or timing gates.
  ☐ Completion: routine frame/resize/startup ownership no longer discards valid intercepted knowledge;
    obsolete purge APIs/callers are removed, complete caller interception gaps are fixed, and intercepted
    effects update authoritative knowledge without unnecessary field-level invalidation/readback. Required
    initial acquisition, operation-outcome uncertainty and restoration remain intact.

Phase 8h: Qualify corrective closure and reconcile documentation
  Depends on 8a–8g.

  ☐ Record before/after routes for every named audit finding and any additional helper-chain occurrence.
    Review actual dependency direction as well as native execution location; demonstrate one admitted
    RHI entry route and existing manager/cache authority for each affected operation family.
  ☐ Run delegated Debug/Release builds and focused behavior regressions on the combined candidate for
    admission, creation failure, acquisition/recovery, dependency publication, labels, suppression,
    context-local tools, retirement and immediate/recorded equivalence. Keep inherited failures explicit;
    integrated lighting/package/user acceptance stays in Phase 9, with no repeated benchmark requirement.
  ☐ Complete second review and independent stage-completion audit against the proposal, contracts,
    every named correction and mandatory migration receipts. Resolve gaps before claiming closure;
    reflection inventories or namespace-only searches do not substitute for caller/dependency review.
  ☐ Reconcile proposal/contracts, migration receipts, inventory/coverage, parent-plan cross-references,
    Rendering.GpuResources.md and Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md with final ownership and actual routes.
    Permanent documentation describes implemented behavior; historical receipts retain their limitations.
  ☐ Completion: all correction units and their caller dependencies satisfy the final RHI boundary with
    retained manager lifetimes, no alternate owner-side execution/acquisition route and no temporary shim.

Phase 8i: Align resource-management service names with RHI ownership
  Depends on 8h; final corrective subphase before integrated qualification.

  ☐ Inventory Gpu-prefixed management, registration and lifecycle services by responsibility. Establish
    an explicit old/new name map, including GpuResourceManager → RHIResourceManager,
    GpuResourceManagerSystem → RHIResourceManagerSystem and GpuResourceDisposalQueue → RHIResourceDisposalQueue.
    Review GpuBindingRegistry, GpuUniformRingSystem and GpuUniformRingFrameController for RHI service
    naming according to their final ownership; do not mechanically rename every Gpu-prefixed type.
    Resource wrappers, canonical metadata and consumer references retain their existing names/contracts.
  ☐ Apply the management-service rename in one coherent sweep across all partial declarations, filenames,
    production and test callers, composition/lifecycle hooks, generated-code inputs and relevant tooling.
    Preserve native execution, initialization/publication authority, context identity, cleanup ordering
    and API behavior. This is a naming correction, not a resource-model or ownership redesign.
  ☐ Update proposal/contracts, inventory/coverage, parent-plan cross-references and permanent architecture
    documentation to match the final names. Keep historical receipts intelligible without rewriting their
    original evidence. Remove superseded names from active APIs/callers; introduce no compatibility alias,
    forwarding class, duplicate service or staged migration shim.
  ☐ Run delegated canonical Debug/ordinary Release builds and focused initialization, registration,
    disposal, context-lifetime and binding regressions after the complete rename. Perform second review
    and independent completion audit, checking symbol/file consistency and unchanged dependency direction.
    No benchmark, timing study, instrumented Release or automatic commit is required or authorized.
  ☐ Completion: management services consistently use uppercase RHI names where their responsibility
    belongs to the RHI, all callers/documentation agree, and retained resource contracts remain unchanged.

Phase 9: Qualify integrated behavior and release artifacts
  Depends on Phase 8, including subphases 8a–8i.
  Outcome: correctness, package identity and outstanding live evidence are explicit.

  ☐ Run delegated focused and integrated regressions on an immutable candidate, covering all family receipts,
    original-context retirement, aliases/views, local tools, context loss, rejected commands, exactly-once
    external calls and immediate/recorded equivalence. Keep hardware coverage gaps explicit.
    Investigate PbrLumOnFullPipelineIntegrationTests' direct-only composite numerical mismatch after
    correcting its depth-comparison inputs and complete MRT declaration. Preserve the independent oracle
    and existing tolerance; do not treat source-boundary qualification as integrated lighting acceptance.
  ☐ Run canonical Debug/Release build and package tooling through subagents; verify installed/package
    identity where the repository workflow requires it. Confirm diagnostic counters remain DEBUG-only.
    Do not create an instrumented Release product.
  ☐ Review storage reuse and allocation sites; verify immediate calls need no recording allocation and
    recording requires no per-command heap object. Use focused behavioral checks for stable binding
    suppression and reuse of available metadata without redundant queries. No timing thresholds or
    before/after benchmark comparison are required for completion.
  ☐ Provide a user-run visual/runtime checklist for graphics, compute, cloud/depth content ordering,
    resize, shader reload and teardown. Record actual user evidence separately from headless tests;
    leave live appearance/runtime-behavior acceptance unchecked until supplied.
  ☐ Reconcile every proposal requirement with implementation and evidence; resolve deviations or obtain
    explicit scope approval rather than declaring unimplemented requirements complete.
  ☐ Completion: all required correctness/package receipts and user-run acceptance are recorded;
    remaining unsupported hardware evidence is disclosed, and no unapproved architecture deviation remains.

Final acceptance
  ☐ All nine phases, their subphases and mandatory migration receipts are satisfied; later cleanup never substitutes for
    removing superseded APIs within their owning migration unit.
  ☐ Final APIs are backend-neutral, managed OpenGL execution requires 4.5, and every native operation has
    one execution owner with existing manager metadata/lifetime authority and preserved cache behavior.
  ☐ GraphicsCommandContext and StateCache's old execution/name surfaces are gone; shader/PSO, geometry,
    targets, uniforms, synchronization and recording follow the approved final contracts.
  ☐ Tests, current-state docs, package verification and user-run evidence support the claimed outcome
    without performance claims or treating headless results as visual acceptance.

Proposal coverage map
  Boundary, operations, capabilities and resource inputs: Phases 1–5; final cache split in Phase 7.
  Creation/adoption, initial content, original identity and retirement: Phases 2–5; corrections in 8; lifecycle tests in 6/9.
  Shader/PSO creation, generation identity, replacement and ownership: Phase 5; recorded rejection in 6.
  Geometry, attachment targets and immediate submission: Phase 5; recorded consumption in 6.
  Uniform capture, physical publication and history: Phase 5; recording/epoch/cancellation tests in 6.
  Recording, ownership transfer, tickets, failure and CPU/GPU completion: Phases 4/6.
  Dependency inversion for manager services, wrappers and semantic RHI objects: subphases 5b–5e and 8a–8h.
  Persistent intercepted-state knowledge and removal of obsolete frame/resize/startup purges: subphase 8g.
  RHI management-service naming and complete caller/documentation reconciliation: subphase 8i.
  Native dispatch, publication, exact integration and no-shim migration: mandatory receipts, Phases 2–8.
  Representative outcomes, efficient-execution checks, package and user-run qualification: Phase 9, supported
    by focused verification and current-state documentation in every owning migration unit.
